<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdullah I of Jordan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdullah I of Jordan, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, was a leader of the Arab Revolt and became emir of Transjordan under the British mandate in 1921. On 25 May 1946 he was proclaimed king of the independent kingdom, renamed Jordan in 1949. His Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> and East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 1948 war, and he was assassinated in Jerusalem in 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullah-i-of-jordan.jpg" alt="Abdullah I of Jordan" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cecil Beaton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_Beaton_Photographs-_Political_and_Military_Personalities;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan;_Abdullah,_King_of_Jordan_CBM1666.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abdullah was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> on 2 February 1882, the second of four sons of Hussein bin Ali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>. In 1910 he persuaded his father to stand for Grand Sharif of Mecca, and in the following year he became deputy for Mecca in the Ottoman parliament. In 1914 he made a clandestine visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a>, seeking British support for his father's ambitions. In 1915 he encouraged his father to correspond with Sir Henry McMahon about Arab independence, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a>.</p><p>During the Revolt of 1916 to 1918 Abdullah commanded the Arab Eastern Army. He began by attacking the Ottoman garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> on 10 June 1916, which he besieged, and then joined the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. In early 1917 he ambushed an Ottoman convoy and captured £20,000 in gold coins meant to bribe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After French forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in July 1920, Abdullah moved his forces from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> into Transjordan intending to liberate it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1921 and persuaded him not to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s French allies. Abdullah established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> on 11 April 1921. A legislative council set up in 1928 was only advisory, and he ruled as an autocrat. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he was a faithful British ally.</p><p>The Hashemite Kingdom of Transjordan was proclaimed independent on 25 May 1946, and Abdullah was crowned king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>; it was renamed Jordan on 26 April 1949. He dreamed of a Greater Syria under a Hashemite throne, which made many Arab states distrust him. On 15 May 1948 he joined the all-Arab intervention, and the Arab Legion fought around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. In 1950 Jordan annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, which angered Syria, Saudi Arabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 20 July 1951 Abdullah was shot dead at the entrance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a>, by Mustafa Shukri Ashu, a Palestinian tailor's apprentice. His grandson Prince Hussein, beside him, survived when a medal deflected a bullet. The assassin was shot by the king's guards. Abdullah was succeeded by his eldest son Talal, and then by Talal's son Hussein.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdullah I of Jordan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Abdullah I of Jordan</b> dreamed of a Greater Syria uniting Syria, Palestine and Jordan under himself; the plan was unpopular among most Arabs, and distrust of his ambitions was one of the principal reasons for founding the Arab League in 1945.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain recognised the independence of the Emirate of Transjordan in 1946, and on 25 May the emir <b>Abdullah I</b> became king; the country was renamed Jordan in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Masjid Al-Aqsa, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abdullah I of Jordan"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5778 5697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5778 5697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
